---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ThermalShockSimulator from '../../components/ThermalShockSimulator.astro';

const pageTitle = "Appliance Thermal Shock & Shatter Threshold Safety Simulator | IsItKitchenSafe";
const pageDescription = "Calculate thermal shock fracture limits, tensile breaking stress, and shatter risk across borosilicate, tempered soda-lime glass, ceramic, and enameled cast iron under rapid temperature swings.";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Why does modern Pyrex shatter more easily than vintage Pyrex?",
    answer: "In 1998, Corning licensed the Pyrex brand name in the United States to World Kitchen (now Corelle Brands), which transitioned manufacturing from borosilicate glass to tempered soda-lime glass. Borosilicate glass has a very low coefficient of thermal expansion (3.3 × 10⁻⁶/°C), tolerating rapid temperature differentials (ΔT) up to 150°C–170°C without breaking. In contrast, tempered soda-lime glass has an expansion coefficient of ~9.0 × 10⁻⁶/°C, making its safe thermal differential only 42°C–50°C. While soda-lime glass is mechanically more resistant to dropping on linoleum, it is nearly three times more vulnerable to thermal shock explosions."
  },
  {
    question: "What exactly causes glass bakeware to explosively detonate rather than just crack?",
    answer: "Tempered glass is produced through thermal prestressing: molten glass is shaped and then rapidly quenched with high-pressure air jets. The exterior solidifies immediately while the molten interior slowly cools and contracts, creating an intense permanent compressive stress envelope (~10,000 PSI) on the surface balanced by high tensile stress in the center core. When a hot dish is placed on a cold or wet surface, the exterior contracts abruptly, adding enormous tensile stress. If this exceeds the compressive surface layer, the massive elastic strain energy stored in the core releases simultaneously across the entire matrix, detonating into thousands of high-velocity projectile fragments (dice sharding) accompanied by a gunshot-like acoustic blast (110–120 dB)."
  },
  {
    question: "Why is putting a hot dish on a cold granite or quartz countertop so dangerous?",
    answer: "Natural granite and engineered quartz countertops have high thermal conductivity (k ≈ 2.8–3.5 W/m·K) and huge thermal mass. When a 200°C (400°F) casserole dish makes direct contact with a 18°C (64°F) stone slab, conductive heat transfer occurs virtually instantaneously across the microscopic contact points. The flat bottom of the dish chills and contracts within seconds, while the interior food and upper walls remain expanded at 200°C. This extreme localized bending moment and shear stress shears the bottom plate right off the dish or triggers a full explosive detonation."
  },
  {
    question: "Can I move a frozen casserole dish directly into a hot oven?",
    answer: "No, never with glass or ceramic stoneware. A frozen casserole stored at -18°C (0°F) placed into a preheated 190°C (375°F) oven creates an instantaneous positive thermal shock differential (ΔT) of over 208°C (375°F). The exterior glaze and outer walls expand rapidly under convective oven air, while the frozen core remains rigid and contracted. This differential expansion tears ceramic stoneware along internal cleavage planes, often causing the dish to split cleanly in half or burst. Only crystalline glass-ceramic (such as Corning Visions or Pyroceram) can safely transition directly from freezer to flame."
  },
  {
    question: "Why does enameled cast iron chip or pop when deglazed with cold liquid?",
    answer: "Enameled cast iron is a composite material made of an iron substrate coated with a thin layer of fused vitreous glass enamel. Cast iron has a thermal expansion coefficient (α ≈ 10.5 × 10⁻⁶/°C) that is significantly higher than that of the glass enamel (α ≈ 8.0 × 10⁻⁶/°C). When an empty or searing hot pot (220°C+) is hit with cold wine or stock straight from the refrigerator (4°C), the iron contracts faster than the enamel. The resulting interfacial shear stress causes the enamel to 'spall' or pop off in razor-sharp microscopic flakes, ruining the pot and contaminating the food with sharp glass particulates."
  },
  {
    question: "How do micro-scratches from utensils reduce cookware thermal shock resistance?",
    answer: "Under fracture mechanics (Griffith's theory of brittle fracture), materials fail when tensile stress at the tip of a microscopic flaw exceeds atomic bond strength. Micro-scratches caused by metal whisks, knives, or harsh scouring pads act as stress concentration risers. A surface flaw just 50 microns deep can amplify localized tensile stress by a factor of 4 to 8. Consequently, a glass baking dish that safely handled a 60°C temperature swing when new may catastrophically fail at just a 35°C swing once scratched."
  }
];

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' },
  { label: 'Thermal Shock Simulator' }
];

const webAppSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "name": "Appliance Thermal Shock & Shatter Threshold Safety Simulator",
  "url": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/thermal-shock-simulator/",
  "applicationCategory": "HealthApplication",
  "operatingSystem": "All",
  "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript. Requires HTML5.",
  "description": pageDescription,
  "offers": {
    "@type": "Offer",
    "price": "0",
    "priceCurrency": "USD"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "IsItKitchenSafe",
    "url": "https://isitkitchensafe.com"
  }
};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} faqs={faqs} availableLocales={['en', 'es', 'pt', 'zh-cn', 'ja']}>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(webAppSchema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
  <div class="bg-surface-soft border-b border-hairline py-1">
    <div class="max-w-[1280px] mx-auto px-6">
      <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} className="mb-0" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-[1200px] mx-auto px-6 py-10 md:py-14">
    <!-- Hero Header -->
    <header class="mb-10 text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <span class="inline-block px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider bg-indigo-600 text-white mb-3">
        Thermodynamic Mechanics &bull; Fracture Dynamics &bull; ASTM C149 Standards
      </span>
      <h1 class="text-display-md md:text-display-lg font-medium text-ink tracking-tight mb-4">
        Appliance Thermal Shock &amp; Shatter Threshold Safety Simulator
      </h1>
      <p class="text-body text-[15px] md:text-body-md leading-relaxed">
        Prevent catastrophic glass bakeware explosions, ceramic crazing, and warped cookware. Calculate exact temperature differential thresholds (ΔT), internal tensile stress, and projectile shatter risks before moving hot dishes.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Main Interactive Simulator -->
    <div class="mb-14">
      <ThermalShockSimulator lang="en" />
    </div>

    <!-- Science Deep-Dive: Physics of Thermal Shock Failures -->
    <section class="mb-14 border-t border-hairline pt-12 space-y-8">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <span class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted block mb-1">
          Coefficient of Thermal Expansion &bull; Tensile Stress &bull; Griffith Flaws
        </span>
        <h2 class="text-title-lg md:text-display-sm font-medium text-ink tracking-tight mb-3">
          The Hidden Physics of Cookware Thermal Explosions
        </h2>
        <p class="text-body text-[15px] leading-relaxed">
          Culinary thermal shock is not a defect of bad luck; it is a predictable mechanical failure governed by the laws of solid-state thermodynamics and brittle fracture mechanics:
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-3">
          <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-xl bg-indigo-600 text-white font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm">
            01
          </div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">Coefficient of Thermal Expansion (CTE)</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Every material expands when heated and contracts when cooled at a rate determined by its CTE (α). Tempered soda-lime glass expands at 9.0 &times; 10⁻⁶/°C, whereas borosilicate glass expands at only 3.3 &times; 10⁻⁶/°C. Higher expansion rates produce proportionally steeper stress gradients when one surface is cooled faster than another.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-3">
          <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-xl bg-amber-600 text-white font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm">
            02
          </div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">Compressive vs. Tensile Envelopes</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Glass is extraordinarily strong under compression (>1,000 MPa) but weak under tension (<100 MPa). Tempering introduces an artificial compressive outer envelope over a high-tension core. Rapid surface cooling creates severe outer contraction, transforming surface compression into catastrophic tensile failure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl bg-surface-soft border border-hairline space-y-3">
          <div class="w-9 h-9 rounded-xl bg-red-600 text-white font-bold flex items-center justify-center text-sm">
            03
          </div>
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-base">Conductive Heat Sinks &amp; Shear Bending</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
            Placing hot bakeware on dense granite, wet towels, or puddle-filled countertops extracts heat by direct conduction hundreds of times faster than ambient air. The bottom contractively pulls inward while the hot upper rims remain expanded, creating severe shear bending moments that rupture the base.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ Section -->
    <section class="border-t border-hairline pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-lg md:text-display-sm font-medium text-ink tracking-tight mb-8">
        Frequently Asked Questions
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl">
        {faqs.map(faq => (
          <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink mb-2">
              {faq.question}
            </h3>
            <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">
              {faq.answer}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
